

GETSOCKNAME(2)      UNIX Programmer's Manual      GETSOCKNAME(2)



NAME
     getsockname - get the local name of a socket

SYNOPSIS
     #include <sys/socket.h>
     #include <netinet/in.h>

     int getsockname(s, name, namelen)
     int s;
     struct sockaddr *name;
     int *namelen;

DESCRIPTION
     Getsockname returns the local address bound to socket s.  On
     entry *namelen must give the size of the buffer name points
     at; on return it holds the number of bytes written, which is
     always sizeof(struct sockaddr_in).

     Unlike getpeername(2) this does not require a connection.  A
     socket that has not been named yet reports the wildcard --
     port 0, and the interface address the machine happens to have
     -- because a program that calls this to discover the port the
     kernel picked has to be able to call it before there is a
     peer.

     The address is 127.0.0.1 for a loopback descriptor and the
     machine's address on its s1 interface otherwise.  The port is
     the socket's own local port: what bind(2) or listen(2) set, or
     for a socket returned by accept(2), the port it was accepted
     ON, which is what BSD reports and what an inetd child expects
     to see.

RETURN VALUE
     0 on success.  On failure -1 is returned and errno is set.

ERRORS
     [EBADF]        s is not a valid descriptor.
     [ENOTSOCK]     s is a file, not a socket.
     [EINVAL]       *namelen is smaller than a sockaddr_in, or
                    namelen is a null pointer.

SEE ALSO
     accept(2), bind(2), getpeername(2), ifconfig(8), socket(2)

BUGS
     The address reported for a wire socket is the interface's
     current address, read when the call is made rather than
     recorded when the socket was bound.  A machine whose lease
     changes under a live connection therefore reports the new
     address for a socket that is still carrying the old one.
     FGX has one interface and static or long-lease addresses, so
     this has not been worth a per-socket copy.



